plan Package
title   Package routes, dependency identity, declarations and installed consumers
status  proposed
goal
    One ESM package whose supported imports, declarations and host boundaries agree with the
    capability plans. This plan owns what a consumer can import and how installed use is proven;
    runtime behaviour stays with its owners.
outcomes
    O1  Routes
        Exactly six routes exist, through explicit package exports and ESM import conditions; no
        CommonJS, wildcard subpath or deep import is supported. Each exposes only these names:

        - flow-state: definition, machine, app, actorRef, runtimeSetup, resource, transaction,
        stream, deny, check, send, FlowError, entity, domain, read, write, listen, eq, oneOf,
        contains, between, overlaps, and, serverMatches, principal, asc, desc; types Definition,
        Machine, App, ActorRef, Actor, Snapshot, Runtime, RuntimeSetup, Diagnostic and Attempt. 
        Attempt is
        also a Schema value carrying id and encoded input (@plan.Operations.O3).
        - flow-state/react: FlowProvider, useActorByRef, useActor, useView, Hydrate.
        - flow-state/testing: story, model, defineConfig, CheckpointProvider, propertyTest; type 
        StoryRun.
        - flow-state/inspect: inspect, render, attachInspector, trace (an object with only import
        and export); types Model and Trace.
        - flow-state/server: dehydrate; type Hydration.
        - flow-state/persistence: layerIndexedDb({ database? }).

        Every other setting or operation is a member of these values, inferred data or an Effect
        primitive. No route re-exports another route's names or internal services, stores,
        ManagedRuntime or mutable harnesses.

        flow-state/react keeps the "use client" directive in its emitted JavaScript; a Next.js
        Server Component may import its components, and hooks run only on the client.
        flow-state/server resolves to the real module under react-server and node conditions and
        to a stub under browser and default, browser winning over a conflicting node condition.
        The stub throws FlowError ServerOnlyImport on evaluation, before any work; it never
        supplies server execution. A non-Node server host selects react-server.

        The root imports no React, server-only or Node code, testing, inspection tooling,
        TypeScript compiler, renderer or IndexedDB, so it runs in request runtimes and edge
        hosts. Every route except react imports without React installed; only rendering 
        CheckpointProvider
        needs React, so a no-React project can load its Story config. Importing the persistence 
        route outside a browser is
        safe; storage access begins only when its Layer is acquired (@plan.Persistence.O1).
    O2  Dependencies and inert imports
        Effect is a peer dependency, never bundled, so Runtime, Schema, Context tags and Layers
        resolve to the consumer's single Effect instance. KeyValueStore comes from
        effect/persistence. React is an optional peer, majors 18 and 19; a host without React
        fails only where it uses the React route or CheckpointProvider.

        package.json declares sideEffects: false. Importing any route constructs no Runtime,
        registers no listener and performs no storage I/O, actor work or user callback. Tree
        shaking never makes a used server-only import succeed in a client bundle.
    O3  Types and removed surfaces
        Packed declarations preserve literal inference, nominal machine and definition identity,
        exact unions and field presence, variance, service requirements and failure channels.
        Consumers need no any, assertions, explicit generic arguments or wrappers. Lanes are
        inferred _tag unions; RuntimeSetup is inferred from the returned setup. Declarations are
        self-contained: no workspace path, internal route or unresolved import escapes, and
        consumers emit isolated declarations without excessive instantiation.

        Removed surfaces are gone, with no alias, overload, adapter or deprecated wrapper. They
        are those in the "Deleted or absorbed" table of references/design/minimal-library.md, plus
        registered views, child and dynamic actors, leases, final/onDone/output, useShallow and
        custom comparators, useResource, callable Stories and mutable harnesses, saved behaviour
        artifacts, compression, Implementation values and the separate error classes
        (FlowUsageError, FlowPersistenceError, FlowDisposeError, FlowStoryExecutionError). A
        configuration object naming a removed or unknown field throws InvalidConfiguration with
        its path and help naming the replacement, before any work starts.
    O4  Installed consumers
        Dispatch Desk is a checked-in application compiled and run against the packed tarball,
        never workspace aliases; it and the seven entity examples become real consumers at S10.
        Its Next.js App Router host proves request render, Hydrate first render without
        mismatch, reload restore, reconnect, disposal, principal isolation and a second tab
        running without persistence. Chromium, Gecko and WebKit run its React lifetimes, real
        IndexedDB, reloads and hydration, with versions recorded.

        Small and medium consumers compile with strict checking, isolatedModules and isolated
        declarations, and packed execution proves each route, including no-React and React
        18 and 19 installs. Every build stage gate runs these packed-consumer checks. The
        installed flow binary resolves the same package identity as the consumer
        (@plan.CommandLine.O6).
decisions
    D1  Six closed routes
        The root keeps the authoring surface; React, server, testing, inspection and storage are
        isolated so a consumer pays only for what it imports.
    D2  Owners define semantics; this plan proves installed reachability
        Package checks preserve owner types and behaviour across publishing and host boundaries;
        they never replace owner proofs.
constraints
    C1  Portable bytes
        Identity, key and structural codecs use ECMAScript and Web APIs (TextEncoder), never Node
        Buffer or UTF-16 counts. Equal accepted data gives equal bytes on Node, Bun, Deno,
        Chromium, Gecko and WebKit; limits belong to @plan.Inspection.O2.
    C2  Generated output
        dist, declarations and docs come from pinned build scripts, never hand edits.
verification
    V1  Packed exports and declarations match the six lists exactly; deep imports, cross-route
        re-exports and every removed name fail; strict isolated consumers compile.
    V2  A no-React consumer imports every route except react; React 18 and 19
        hosts run; one Effect identity is shared; "use client" survives bundling; browser
        bundles reach ServerOnlyImport; import spies see no work.
    V3  Codec vectors give equal bytes on every listed host; removed configuration fields
        throw InvalidConfiguration before any side effect.
    V4  Packed Dispatch Desk passes its Next.js, isolation, reload, uncertainty and second-tab
        gates in Chromium, Gecko and WebKit.
questions
    Q1  Which Effect peer range is published?
        Recommended: exactly 4.0.5, widened only after packed-consumer proofs on each range.
    Q2  Which browser releases form the gate baseline?
        Recommended: the versions the pinned harness supplies at S10, one each of Chromium,
        Gecko and WebKit.
